From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm exl3: TF_GLM_OVN_X3_NC, X3_XROW and X3_FUSEDOWN, production's decode kernels on the item plan

Ported from our archived fork `ai-engine` (ref `fork-ssh/ovn-up`): 7d41fa9b7 "TF_GLM_OVN_X3_*" (the 16-byte staged
trellis loads, the fused down epilogue, the row rotation), ca8479639 (the walker's variants as flags inside the plain
k2 switch) and the item-plan plumbing of e95d31f81 / 769c8cfca (standard-mode members in nc_body; the 9-slot decode
arena, which 0012 already has here). Hand ported: the pin keeps GroupedArgs and grouped_body in exl3_grouped.cuh
with a LOAD template argument (upstream's load-ahead, 8d46e2906), and a decode window here always runs on the item
plan (0012). The 16-byte staged loads follow TensorFold's glm5_next/cuda/exl3.cu dec_kernel LD 1 (Apache-2.0;
jayleaton 0580), which production serves.

Left out, and why:
- 95a3e51cb, TF_GLM_OVN_X3_ROTEXPERT: a per-expert column share, a numerics trade; and the fork's one-word walker
  (X3_NC=w), which production does not run.
- The fork's tf_exl3_ovn_rot_rows and ldg_nc_v4 asm: upstream v1.2.7 already has both. tf_exl3_prompt_rotate
  (exl3_experts.cu, the prompt path's shared_input rotation) is the same arithmetic on bf16 rows, so X3_XROW launches
  it on the window's rows; tf_common::ld_nc (common/cp_async.cuh) is the same ld.global.nc.L1::no_allocate.v4.
  Upstream has no 16-byte loads, fused down or row rotation in the routed decode, no trellis base per projection and
  no X3 knob (git grep at 93a2e93ec, work/verify-gap/upstream-overlap.sh).

What each knob does on a decode window's routed experts (prompt chunks untouched), all default off, each read once
through cuda/ovn.zig from a .glm row of platform/environment.zig:
- TF_GLM_OVN_X3_NC=1|2 (u64, at most 2): gate/up and down launch tf_exl3_ovn_nc1|2 (exl3_ovn.cuh nc_body): the
  expert, count and member codes in one round trip, then each warp's trellis in 16-byte read-only loads issued before
  the member barrier and kept 1 or 2 k steps ahead, staged through the warp's 1 KB of the reduction buffer. It applies
  where the layer is 4-bit mcg with verified widths, each expert's trellis sits at base + e * its words (the loader's
  layout; cuda_exl3.load now keeps the three bases) and a warp's k steps divide by the depth (exl3_experts.variant);
  elsewhere the launch keeps the walker below or the plain kernel. GLM-5.3-Flash takes both depths at every share.
- TF_GLM_OVN_X3_XROW=1: where every expert's gate and up input share one rotation (the loader's shx) and the rows are
  bf16, one rotation a token row (tf_exl3_prompt_rotate, rows x d/128 programs) instead of one per pick and matrix
  (rows x 9 x d/128 x 2), and gate and up read row p / slots of it.
- TF_GLM_OVN_X3_FUSEDOWN=1: where the down runs in one K split (GLM's always does), the down launch writes ey's slot
  rows itself through tf_exl3_down_combine's per-slot arithmetic (0 + the warp sum, the rotation, * svh), so
  tf_exl3_down_combine and the down's Z round trip go.
A launch with XROW or FUSEDOWN on and no 16-byte loads takes tf_exl3_ovn_walk: grouped_body with the row index and
the fused epilogue, its LOAD the plain kernel's (GROUPED_LOAD).

Bits: every variant performs the plain launch's mma in the same k order on the same decoded words (a 4-bit lane reads
words lane + 31 and lane, the shuffle reproducing lane_start<8>), sums the four warps in the same order and stores the
same float; the fused epilogue is down_combine's expression at sk 1; the row rotation is rot_in's bf16 expression
with every expert's equal suh. Knobs off is today's launch sequence and arguments exactly: no tail is added and the
same functions launch with the same arguments.

Why (lane VerifyGap, work/verify-gap/REPORT.md): like for like against production at 2 and 4 streams the recipe is
-9.4% / -10.6%, and the hot frame of rank 0's round is the routed grouped GEMM, tf_exl3_grouped_1, 30.20 / 52.35 ms of
52.74 / 80.51 ms (~/nsys/rec-p2.sqlite, rec-p4.sqlite). Production serves this kernel path; the fork measured our
item-plan routed path 3 to 7% behind production's on one GPU and the X3 set matching it (ai-engine docs/PARITY.md
828-852), and +4.5 to +4.9% decode at 1 to 8 streams on the MiaAI recipe with equal digests (SPEEDUPS.md 154-157),
before upstream's load-ahead (#1505, +0.5 to 0.7%). Expected here: about -1.1 to -2.0 ms a round at 2 streams and
-1.9 to -3.2 ms at 4, to be measured in both orders.

Receipts (lane VerifyGap, 2026-11-14; base = v1.2.7 + 0001..0033, head = base + this patch):
